detailedExplanation.whatItIs:
useEffect takes a function (the effect) and an optional dependency array:
useEffect(() => {
  document.title = `Count: ${count}`;
}, [count]);
The effect runs after the component renders, and re-runs whenever count changes.

If your effect sets something up, you return a function that tears it down:
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(tick, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
The returned function is the cleanup.

useMemo takes a function and a dependency array and returns the memoized result:
const sorted = useMemo(() => bigList.sort(compare), [bigList]);
It recomputes sorted only when bigList changes; otherwise it returns the cached value.

useCallback wraps a function so its reference is preserved across renders:
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doThing(id), [id]);
handleClick keeps the same identity until id changes.

A custom hook is a function like:
function useToggle(initial = false) {
  const [on, setOn] = useState(initial);
  const toggle = () => setOn(o => !o);
  return [on, toggle];
}
Components call const [open, toggleOpen] = useToggle().

Derived state is any value that is a pure function of other values you already have. For example, given todos and a filter, the visibleTodos list is derived. You can compute it inline:
const visible = todos.filter(t => matches(t, filter));
rather than keeping a separate visibleTodos state.

A common pattern uses one state object and a shared handler:
const [form, setForm] = useState({ name: '', email: '' });
const handleChange = e => setForm(f => ({ ...f, [e.target.name]: e.target.value }));
Each input has a name and value tied to that object.

An error boundary is a class component that implements getDerivedStateFromError and/or componentDidCatch:
static getDerivedStateFromError(error) { return { hasError: true }; }
When a child throws during render, the boundary catches it and can render a fallback like 'Something went wrong.'

You create a portal with ReactDOM.createPortal:
return ReactDOM.createPortal(<Modal />, document.getElementById('modal-root'));
The Modal renders into modal-root in the DOM, even though the code lives deep inside another component.

A basic pattern fetches in an effect:
useEffect(() => {
  let active = true;
  fetch(url).then(r => r.json()).then(d => { if (active) setData(d); });
  return () => { active = false; };
}, [url]);
State tracks data, loading, and error.

useReducer returns the state and a dispatch function:
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState);
You update by dispatching actions: dispatch({ type: 'increment' }). The reducer decides how each action transforms state.

You compute the class string from conditions:
const cls = `btn ${isActive ? 'btn-active' : ''}`;
or with a helper:
className={clsx('btn', { 'btn-active': isActive, 'btn-disabled': disabled })}
The element gets whichever classes apply.

You wrap a component:
const Row = React.memo(function Row({ item }) { ... });
Now Row re-renders only when its props change by shallow comparison, not just because its parent re-rendered.
